As an alternative, using NSRegularExpression would be cleaner and closer to the original
 regex intent:

```swift
public func extractTags(from text: String) -> [String] {
    let pattern = try! NSRegularExpression(pattern: "#([A-Za-z0-9_-]+)")
    let ns = text as NSString
    let matches = pattern.matches(in: text, range: NSRange(location: 0, length: ns
.length))
    return matches.compactMap { match in
        guard let r = Range(match.range(at: 1), in: text) else { return nil }
        return String(text[r])
    }
}
```

NSRegularExpression is fine on macOS. Both work. A manual scan has no regex dependency
 and is deterministic, but NSRegularExpression is more concise and its "immediately
 followed by" semantics are clear. Either is fine. I'll go with the manual scan —
 it avoids the force-try, and the character-class semantics are explicit. Actually
